body:
The bounds clamping on line 334 uses `.min(text.len())` for `start_byte`, but this could still result in an invalid range if `start_byte` ends up in the middle of a multi-byte UTF-8 character.

For example:
- Text contains "café" at position 0 (é is 2 bytes: 0xC3 0xA9)
- If LSP sends position pointing to byte 4 (middle of é)
- `start_byte = 4` is not a character boundary
- `text.replace_range(4..old_end_byte, ...)` will panic with "byte index 4 is not a char boundary"

Consider rounding to the nearest character boundary:
```rust
let start_byte = {
    let raw = lsp_position_to_byte_offset(...).min(text.len());
    // Round down to char boundary
    (0..=raw).rev().find(|&i| text.is_char_boundary(i)).unwrap_or(0)
};
```
```suggestion
                        let raw_start = crate::convert::lsp_position_to_byte_offset(
                            text,
                            start_line,
                            start_character,
                        )
                        .min(text.len());
                        // Round start_byte down to the nearest UTF-8 char boundary.
                        let start_byte = (0..=raw_start)
                            .rev()
                            .find(|&i| text.is_char_boundary(i))
                            .unwrap_or(0);

                        let raw_old_end = crate::convert::lsp_position_to_byte_offset(
                            text,
                            end_line,
                            end_character,
                        )
                        .min(text.len())
                        // Ensure the raw end is not before the raw start.
                        .max(raw_start);
                        // Round old_end_byte down to the nearest UTF-8 char boundary,
                        // but never before start_byte.
                        let old_end_byte = (start_byte..=raw_old_end)
                            .rev()
                            .find(|&i| text.is_char_boundary(i))
                            .unwrap_or(start_byte);
